# Unity tests

The Unity suite in `test_inventatory_scan_core.cpp` (and the files beside it) is
compiled into firmware for the Scan R1 ESP32-S3. The same suite also runs on a
desktop computer, which is how CI executes the assertions.

## Run on a desktop (no hardware)

From the repository root:

```sh
make -C host-tests test
```

The host build is described in `host-tests/README.md`. It also runs
`host-tests/tests/`, which needs the NVS stand-in for power-loss and write-failure
tests.

## Build the embedded image (no hardware)

The hosted CI job builds this test image but does not upload it or execute its
assertions:

```sh
pio test -e waveshare_esp32s3_nano_usb --without-uploading --without-testing
```

## Run the assertions on a board

This uploads the image to an attached Scan R1 and runs it. It is a manual step that
needs explicit authorization:

```sh
pio test -e waveshare_esp32s3_nano_usb
```
